night progresses, the atmosphere grows increasingly tense, despite the festivities. The clock’s chimes serve as a grim reminder of time’s relentless march, and the guests, though masked and disguised, are haunted by an undercurrent of dread. The turning point arrives at midnight, when a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in blood and wearing a corpse-like mask. The figure moves silently through the seven chambers, inciting terror and disbelief. Prince Prospero, enraged by the intrusion and the violation of his sanctuary, draws a dagger and pursues the phantom through the rooms, driven by fury and a desperate need to reclaim control. The chase leads them into the black chamber, where the figure finally turns to face him—revealing not a living man, but an empty shell, a hollow embodiment of the plague that has infiltrated even the most fortified refuge.\\n\\nIn that moment, Prospero collapses dead, and the truth dawns on the horrified guests: the Red Death was never an intruder—it was the inevitable consequence of their denial and arrogance. The plague had already claimed them all, and the figure was merely a manifestation of their collective fate. As the lights flicker and fade, the final chamber is consumed by darkness, and every soul within the abbey perishes, their lives extinguished in silence. The story ends with the complete annihilation of the isolated elite, underscoring the futility of attempting to escape death through wealth, power, or isolation. The narrative concludes not with triumph, but with the chilling realization that no sanctuary, no matter how secure, can withstand the universal reach of mortality.\",\n  1449→      \"themes\": [\n  1450→        \"identity\",\n  1451→        \"ambition\",\n  1452→        \"loss\"\n  1453→      ],\n  1454→      \"narrative_style\": \"first-person retrospective\"\n  1455→    },\n  1456→    \"model_usage\": {\n  1457→      \"Chapter Detection\": {\n  1458→        \"model\": \"qwen3:30b-